<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kazan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kazan is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> at its confluence with the Kazanka, the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatarstan">Republic of Tatarstan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Founded as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> fortress, it became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanate-of-kazan">Khanate of Kazan</a> in the 15th century and was stormed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> in 1552, a victory commemorated by St Basil's Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. It remains the centre of Volga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> culture, and its kremlin is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kazan.jpg" alt="Kazan" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vitaly Ilyin · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Дворец_земледельцев2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kazan lies at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and the Kazanka rivers. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> a kazan is a cauldron, and the name perhaps describes the topographic basin around the city. Its central part is divided by the Bolaq canal and Kaban Lakes into two districts: the Kazan Posad, historically Russian, on the hill, and the Old Tatar Quarter, historically Tatar, between the canal and the Volga, where the mosques stand. Its symbol is Zilant, a mythical dragon-like creature, and the first official coat of arms was approved on 18 October 1781.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to the official version adopted today, the city is more than 1,000 years old, with an estimated date of the urban settlement of 1004 to 1005. The dating rests on finds in the Kazan Kremlin, among them a Czech coin, remains of masonry and a wooden city fence, and handicrafts.</p><p>In 1552 Tsar Ivan the Terrible conquered the city after a siege. During the governorship of Alexander Gorbaty-Shuysky most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> residents were forcibly Christianised or deported, and mosques and palaces were ruined. The survivors were moved 50 kilometres away, and Tatars in Russian service were settled in the Tatar Bistäse near the city wall. Several great fires destroyed much of the city, and after one of them, in 1579, the icon Our Lady of Kazan was discovered there.</p><p>In 1708 the Tsardom of Kazan was abolished and Kazan became the seat of Kazan Governorate. After a visit by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> it became a centre of shipbuilding for the Caspian fleet. Pugachev's Rebellion, which began in 1773, seized and largely destroyed the city, which was rebuilt under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a>.</p><p>In 1918 Kazan was the capital of the Idel-Ural State, which the Bolshevik government suppressed, and in August of that year the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovak-legion">Czechoslovak Legions</a> briefly occupied it. In 1920 it became the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatar-assr">Tatar Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic</a>. From the Treaty of Rapallo of 1922 until 1933 the German and Russian armies jointly ran the Kama tank school there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kazan is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatarstan">Tatarstan</a>, with over 1.3 million inhabitants, and the most populous city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>. It is known for the mix of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> and Russian cultures. The Kazan Kremlin, a citadel declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 2000, holds the five-domed Annunciation Cathedral of 1561 to 1562 and the Söyembikä Tower, named after the last queen of Kazan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kazan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its successor states, the khanates of <b>Kazan</b>, Crimea, Astrakhan and Sibir, carried its political traditions into the early modern period, the Crimean Khanate surviving until 1783, and the Volga and Crimean Tatars, the Kazakhs and the Uzbeks all trace parts of their history to the Ulus of Jochi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Furs, wax, honey and leather were the main exports, traded through Novgorod to the Hanseatic League, down the Volga to <b>Kazan</b> and the steppe, and south towards the Crimea, where Moscow's merchants known as surozhane dealt in silks and other luxuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>No document of the khanate survived the Russian conquest, and the records of the early Russian administration of <b>Kazan</b> were destroyed during the Time of Troubles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khanate-of-kazan">Khanate of Kazan · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The early years of Ivan’s reign brought reforms by his circle of advisers, and in 1552 his army stormed <b>Kazan</b>, followed by the annexation of the Khanate of Astrakhan in 1556.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The centre of the region later shifted to <b>Kazan</b>, capital of the Khanate of Kazan from the fifteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>European scholars wrote early grammars and dictionaries of the language, among them Abel Rémusat in Paris in 1820, Isaak Jakob Schmidt in St Petersburg in 1831 and Osip Kovalevskiĭ, whose Mongol-Russian-French dictionary appeared in <b>Kazan</b> in 1844–1849.</blockquote><a href="/language/mongolian">Mongolian · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
